      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. As written, it looks unsafe.\n\n- **The dose is too high.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum is 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, depending on the formulation. At this dose the risk of serious harm, including lactic acidosis, is high, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **The prescriber is unclear.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders normally need to come from a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient.\n\nA few ways to move forward:\n1. A prescriber can confirm the intended dose. Common regimens are 500 mg, 850 mg, or 1000 mg two or three times daily with meals, up to 2,550 mg/day.\n2. If the intended dose was 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day), or 1700 mg/day split into doses, I can enter that once it's confirmed.\n3. If the order is correct as written, the prescriber should enter it or tell me directly, and pharmacy should review it.\n\nWhich would you like to do?"
      }
     ],
